<p>Peruvian police raided properties linked to former national election chief Piero Corvetto as prosecutors widened an investigation into alleged irregularities in the 2026 general election, while vote counting remained unresolved nearly two weeks after the ballot. Corvetto resigned this week, and authorities collected material from his home and from other election officials as public frustration and political tensions grew over the slow, disputed tally. The new probe has put Peru’s election system under even sharper scrutiny at a moment when the country is still trying to determine its next leader.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ASgtiKNMPZVlK1-NT1Qx3dNvBiIIbWrJBBERM4mRQK2TGGFNBSrjaMQQGEZirjRGaNerISZejiJY_fyAb0qfw2koLA0hEFXpz36peKp_3wAmvyd7jO-Y0gliO_Vxb79al4TqHTIN4g-cXyYrToeNUDKTNC_RRctHFrSbsDFxrQ' alt='Police activity linked to the widening investigation into Peru&#x27;s election administration.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Ballot shortages: Investigators are also examining reports that some polling stations ran short of ballots, widening the inquiry beyond the delayed count itself.</li><li>Leadership vacuum: Corvetto’s resignation added pressure on Peru’s election administration while vote counting and challenges remained unresolved.</li><li>Fraud claims: The Financial Times reported that accusations of manipulation spread across the political system as parties and voters waited for final figures two weeks after ballots were cast.</li><li>Evidence search: Police collected material from the homes of Corvetto and other officials as part of an anticorruption investigation tied to the election process.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Peruvian police and prosecutors: Authorities widened the case from delayed vote counting to alleged electoral irregularities, using raids to gather documents and other material. (<a href='https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/peruvian-police-raids-property-former-electoral-chief-amid-irregularities-2026-04-24/?ab-singular=2'>Reuters</a>)</li><li>Piero Corvetto: The former election chief stepped down this week as criticism mounted over the pace of the count, bringing even more scrutiny to his role. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/d1f1185a-0521-4196-b07c-00e100b05183'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Public critics and political actors: Outcry over the delayed tally and suspicions of manipulation turned the count into a political flashpoint, increasing pressure on election institutions to explain what happened. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/24/police-raid-perus-election-authorities-after-outcry-over-slow-vote-count'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/24/police-raid-perus-election-authorities-after-outcry-over-slow-vote-count'>Police raid Peru’s election authorities after outcry over slow vote count</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/d1f1185a-0521-4196-b07c-00e100b05183'>Peru grapples with selecting new leader amid ongoing election chaos</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/peru-police-raid-election-chiefs-home-ballot-shortages-132356177'>Peru police raid ex-election chief’s home as ballot shortages spark a widening probe</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/peruvian-police-raids-property-former-electoral-chief-amid-irregularities-2026-04-24/?ab-singular=2'>National Police of Peru officials raid the home of Peru&#x27;s former chief electoral official as part of a ​new probe into alleged electoral irregularities, after he resigned this week over delays in counting ‌votes from the 2026 Peruvian general election.</a> - reuters.com</li></ul>